# schedule/ROSTER -- the single source scheduler#79 is building toward.
# project | account@host | rate | state
#
# THE ONLY SURFACE THAT ARMS A PROJECT (2026-08-30, #364). It used to merely
# OUTRANK _paced.<host>.conf's enabled column -- that column answered for any
# project this file did not name, so which surface decided depended on which
# file had heard of the project. usage-paced-runner.sh's participant_enabled
# now takes <name> <host> and is never handed a conf column at all, so a row
# this file does not name does not dispatch, and says so in run.log.
#
# _paced.<host>.conf is still the ROTATION SOURCE in account mode -- which
# rows exist and what command each runs. Deleting it is #364, gated on host
# mode having really dispatched from here.
# WITNESS: tests/paced-roster-authority-witness.sh.
#
# `dose <project> --arm` / `--park` (#291) is the only supported way to
# change `state`. It opens a PR with auto-merge armed, so a person MERGES and
# the verb WRITES -- it does not hand-edit this file. It refuses a uid
# 3000-3099 self-dev caller, and refuses a project with no unix account on
# the row's host, so no project can arm itself and arming cannot converge to
# a break. (#521: an earlier header said "ONLY A HUMAN EDITS THIS FILE'S
# `state` COLUMN", read cold as "a person types the change"; that is false
# and misled an agent into reporting it as fact on 2026-09-03.)
#
# A new project is REGISTERED first -- a `parked` row plus
# `schedule/<project>.conf`, and the unix account provisioned -- and only
# then armed. `--arm` on an absent project reports `GAP`; it does not create
# the row.
#
# `state` is ONE field (live/parked) -- the whole point, per #79: it is what
# makes "enabled=1 in one file but EXEMPT commented in another" unrepresentable.
# `rate` was 6h for every row -- the value the migration inherited from
# _runner.monkey.conf so that it changed nothing. RAISED TO 2h for the live
# rows on 2026-08-12, Zach-directed ("up the arithmetic to increase pace").
#
# THE ARITHMETIC OF THE ASK, so the next reader can redo it rather than trust
# it. PACED_MAX_PER_TICK=1 and each account can only execute its OWN row (the
# other homes are 0700), so a tick is at most one dispatch per account:
#
#   6h  ->  4 ticks/day x 5 =  20 asks/day   (before 2026-08-12, 5 live rows)
#   2h  -> 12 ticks/day x 5 =  60 asks/day   (that raise, 3x)
#   2h/6h mixed, 10 live    =  96 asks/day   (7x12 + 3x4, until 2026-08-16)
#   20m -> 72 ticks/day x 10 = 720 asks/day  (now -- but see the CEILING block
#                              below: an ask is no longer a dispatch, and the
#                              dispatches land at ~142/day, not 720)
#
# Note PACED_MAX_PER_TICK is NOT a lever here: it is capped by the number of
# rows an account can actually run, which is one. Under host dispatch
# (hf7y/scheduler#55) it becomes a real knob; today only the interval is.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ONLY PLACE THE RATE IS SET. _runner.<host>.conf's RUNNER_CRON
# is deliberately left alone: it is the legacy writer, applied by running
# `sync-crontab.sh --apply` AS EACH ACCOUNT, which is the five-way manual dance
# hf7y/scheduler#81 exists to retire. Editing both would have made two writers
# for one fact, and whichever ran last would win silently -- exactly
# hf7y/scheduler#119. Change the rate HERE and converge with the verb:
#
#     dose <project> --apply        (on the host named in the row)
#
# WHAT THE GATE SAID WHEN THIS WAS SET, measured not assumed:
#   verdict=RUN binding=7d util=0.310 burnline=0.258 slack=-0.052
#   resets_in_min=7482
# 31% of the 7-day window used with 1.8 days elapsed -- ALREADY ABOVE the
# even-burn line, and running only because USAGE_RUSH_BEFORE_RESET_MIN=10080
# disables pacing for the whole window. At 3x, the 0.99 ceiling is reachable
# in roughly a day, after which the gate HOLDS until the window rolls. That is
# the ceiling working as configured (Zach, 2026-07-29), not a failure -- but it
# means the observable outcome of this change may be "burns hard, then pauses"
# rather than "burns evenly". Dial the rate here if that is not wanted.
#
# ===========================================================================
# `rate` IS A CEILING, NOT A SETPOINT (2026-08-16, hf7y/scheduler#219)
# ===========================================================================
# It used to be the pace itself: whatever interval a row named was the interval
# the account dispatched at, so changing a project's pace meant a human editing
# a row here. That happened this morning -- realisateur 2h -> 30m, by hand,
# because its queue had grown to 63 open issues -- and it is the last time it
# should have to.
#
# bin/tempo.sh now derives each project's interval from the work it can
# actually do:
#
#   actionable = open issues MINUS those labelled needs-human/deferred/blocked/question
#   want_min   = clamp( 120 * 12 / max(1, actionable), 20, 1440 )
#
# and usage-paced-runner.sh holds the row until that many minutes have passed
# since its last DISPATCH. So the two numbers do different jobs:
#
#   this file  -> how often the account may ASK          (the ceiling, cron)
#   tempo      -> how many of those asks become work     (the setpoint)
#
# ALL LIVE ROWS ARE 20m FOR THAT REASON, and it is not a fleet-wide speed-up.
# A ceiling coarser than the setpoint quantises it: at a 30m ask, tempo's 34
# min answer for realisateur becomes 60 min of real spacing, i.e. the hand edit
# would have been UNDONE by switching the thermostat on. 20m is not a taste
# call either -- it is TEMPO_MIN_MIN, the fastest pace tempo can ever ask for,
# so the ceiling is derived from a knob rather than picked.
#
# WHAT EACH ROW ACTUALLY PACES AT, measured 2026-08-16 by running
# `bin/tempo.sh <project>` against every live row -- this is its output, not a
# calculation, and `bin/tempo.sh <project>` reprints it on demand:
#
#   realisateur    42 actionable -> 34 min  -> ~36/day    (was 48 at the 30m ask)
#   wtul           26 -> 55 min             -> ~24/day
#   gardien        24 -> 60 min             -> ~24/day
#   scheduler      15 -> 96 min             -> ~14/day
#   groc-mangr     11 -> 131 min            -> ~10/day
#   bibliothecaire 10 -> 144 min            -> ~9/day
#   ecosim          8 -> 180 min            -> ~8/day
#   nine-speakers   6 -> 240 min            -> ~6/day
#   sequestria      6 -> 240 min            -> ~6/day
#   vim-arcade      5 -> 288 min            -> ~5/day
#
#   fleet ~142 attempts/day vs 96 before, but the 7d quota gate is unchanged
#   and already binding at 88%, so what actually moves is the SHARE: 25% of
#   dispatch to realisateur against 24% of the fleet's actionable backlog.
#
# TO CHANGE THE FLEET'S PACE, EDIT TEMPO_BASE_MIN in schedule/_tempo.conf, not
# a row here. One knob moves every project proportionally and needs no --apply.
# Editing a rate here now only decides how coarse that project's ceiling is.
#
# ORDER OF OPERATIONS, because getting it backwards is a runaway: tempo must be
# present in an account's clone BEFORE its ceiling is raised. A clone still on
# an older commit runs a dispatcher with no regulator, and would take the 20m
# ask literally. Check `ls bin/tempo.sh` in the account's checkout, then
# `dose <project> --apply`.
#
# WHAT THE GATE SAID WHEN THE CEILINGS WERE RAISED, measured not assumed:
#   verdict=RUN binding=7d ceiling=0.9 util=0.880 burnline=0.903 slack=+0.023
#   resets_in_min=975
# 2 points of headroom with 16h to the roll. Expect the first day to be "wins
# most of the remaining grants, then everyone holds", not 4x more work.
#
# Seeded 2026-08-11 from _paced.monkey.conf and each schedule/<p>.conf's
# CRON_HOST/CRON_ACCOUNT. That seeding is history: this file is the authority
# now, not a derivation of those, and nothing corroborates it against them.

